// ora-mod-ppm — generated by tools/merge.js: src/ora-mod-ppm-*.js plus matching questions from the Oracle role banks (tools/modules.js).
// Projects Integration for Finance — what a Finance lead must know about Fusion Project Financial Management.
// Part 1: suite basics, chart of accounts, costing sources and accounting.
QB['ora-mod-ppm'] = (QB['ora-mod-ppm'] || []).concat([
['PPM Basics', 'What is in Oracle Fusion Project Financial Management, and how does it connect to Financials?', `**Why they ask:** a Finance lead on a construction or project client must know the suite even without implementing it.

**Answer:**
"The suite has Project Foundation for project types, templates, expenditure types and rates; Project Costing, which collects every cost by project, task and expenditure type; Project Control for budgets, forecasts and commitments; Project Contracts and Billing for invoices and revenue; and Project Asset Management for capital projects. It connects to Financials at every step: costs come from Payables, Receipt Accounting, Cost Accounting, Expenses and Time; invoices go to Receivables; revenue and burden accounting go to GL through Subledger Accounting; and capital projects send assets to Fixed Assets."

Tip: Draw it as a hub: costs in from AP, receipts, inventory and time; invoices out to AR; accounting to GL; assets to FA.`, 'B', 'M', null, {t:1, f:['Which of these modules create their own accounting?', 'Project Costing accounts burden costs and its own adjustments; Project Billing accounts revenue and invoices. Costs from AP, receipts or inventory are accounted by those modules, not again by Projects.']}],
['Project vs SCM Costing', 'How is Project Costing different from SCM Cost Accounting?', `**Why they ask:** people mix them up, especially in construction and engineer-to-order.

**Answer:**
"They answer different questions. SCM Cost Accounting asks what an item costs: it values inventory in a cost organization and cost book using standard, average or FIFO, and handles receipts, work in process, cost of goods sold and variances. Project Costing asks what a job has cost so far: it collects cost by project, task and expenditure type from any source, adds burden, compares to budget and feeds billing, revenue and capitalization. Where they meet, material issued to a project is valued by Cost Accounting and then transferred to Project Costing. Each cost is accounted once, by the module that created it."

Tip: One line to remember: Cost Accounting values the item; Project Costing totals the job.`, 'B', 'M', null, {t:1, f:['Does Project Costing create revenue?', 'No. Project Costing is cost only. Revenue and invoices come from Project Billing working with Project Contracts; together they give margin by job.']}],
['PPM Basics', 'Explain expenditure item, expenditure type, expenditure category and transaction source.', `**Why they ask:** basic vocabulary every Projects conversation uses.

**Answer:**
"An expenditure item is one cost line on a project: project, task, expenditure type, organization, date, quantity and amount. The expenditure type describes the cost, like Subcontractor, Material or Labor, and usually drives the natural account. Expenditure categories group types, such as Labor or Materials, for budgets and reporting. Revenue categories group types for revenue. The transaction source tells you where the cost came from, such as Payables, Receipt Accounting, Inventory or Time, and whether it was already accounted."

Tip: Mention that expenditure type usually decides the natural account in SLA.`, 'B', 'M', null, {f:['Why does the transaction source matter for accounting?', 'It tells Projects whether the cost is already accounted. Payables or receipt costs are imported as accounted, so Projects does not post them again.']}],
['PPM Basics', 'What do project types and templates control from a Finance point of view?', `**Why they ask:** these settings decide what gets capitalized, billed and burdened.

**Answer:**
"The project type decides whether a project is billable, capitalizable, sponsored or indirect, and whether burdening applies and which burden schedule. Templates hold defaults like organization, tasks, billable and capitalizable flags, and transaction controls that limit which expenditure types can be charged. For Finance this matters because it drives whether costs go to WIP, CIP or expense, whether they can be billed, and how overhead is applied."

Tip: Mention transaction controls. They prevent wrong costs hitting a job.`, 'B', 'M', null, {f:['Who should own project types and templates?', 'Finance and the PMO together, with changes controlled, because they drive accounting, billing and capitalization.']}],
['Chart of Accounts', 'Is a project segment required in the chart of accounts? Why or why not?', `**Why they ask:** a classic design question for project-heavy clients.

**Answer:**
"Usually not. Projects already stores every cost, bill and revenue line by project and task, and you can drill from GL to the project transaction. A project segment means a new value, hierarchy update and cross-validation upkeep for every job, and hundreds of short-lived jobs bloat code combinations and the balances cube. Instead I keep the chart for stable dimensions and carry the project number as an SLA supporting reference. I'd only add a job segment, or job as a second balancing segment, if there's a statutory or joint-venture need for a balance sheet by job; even then, a project type or line-of-business segment is often enough."

Tip: Say the principle: the chart holds what's stable; volatile detail lives in the subledger.`, 'D', 'S', null, {t:1, f:['But cost centers and accounts also run into hundreds. Why is project different?', 'Cost centers and accounts are long-lived and slow to change. Jobs open and close every week, so the upkeep and the growth of code combinations never stop.']}],
['Chart of Accounts', 'Finance wants a trial balance by project. How do you deliver it without a project segment?', `**Why they ask:** the natural follow-up to the segment question.

**Answer:**
"I define Project as an SLA supporting reference, derived from the source transaction, and attach it to journal line rules for Project Costing, Project Billing, Payables, Receivables and Cost Management, with balances maintained. SLA then keeps balances by account and project and carries balance sheet accounts forward. The Supporting Reference Balances and Account Analysis by Supporting Reference reports give a trial balance by project for every project-related account: cost, WIP or CIP, revenue, unbilled, billings in excess, AR, retention and AP. It won't net to zero by job because cash and equity aren't tracked by job; if they need self-balancing books by job, that's when job becomes a balancing segment."

Tip: Clarify first: project P&L plus project balances, or truly self-balancing books by job?`, 'D', 'S', null, {t:1, f:['What about manual GL journals for a project?', 'Plain GL journals bypass SLA, so they can\'t carry the reference. Project adjustments should be entered in Projects as miscellaneous costs, adjustments or transfers so they flow through SLA with the project.']}],
['Costing Sources', 'How does an AP invoice cost reach Project Costing?', `**Why they ask:** the most common integration a Finance lead supports.

**Answer:**
"The invoice distribution carries project, task, expenditure type, expenditure organization and expenditure item date. Payables validates and accounts the invoice, so the GL entry comes from AP. Then Import Costs, with Payables as the source, brings the distributions into Project Costing as expenditure items. Because they arrive already accounted, Projects doesn't post them again; it only adds project detail and any burden on top."

Tip: Say "accounted once, by the module that created it."`, 'B', 'M', null, {t:1, f:['An AP cost is missing from the project. What do you check?', 'Whether the invoice is validated and accounted, whether Import Costs ran for Payables, and the import exceptions for errors like a closed task or a transaction control blocking the expenditure type.']}],
['Costing Sources', 'How do requisitions and purchase orders show up in Projects?', `**Why they ask:** tests the difference between commitments and actuals.

**Answer:**
"Requisitions and POs with project details aren't costs; Projects reads them as commitments, so the project sees what is ordered but not yet spent. That feeds budget versus actual, forecasts and budgetary control. Actual cost comes later: for expense items accrued at receipt, the receipt accrual from Receipt Accounting is imported; for period-end accrual or no-receipt items, the AP invoice is imported; for inventory items, cost moves to the project only when material is issued to the job."

Tip: Commitments plus actuals versus budget is how project managers judge exposure.`, 'D', 'M', null, {f:['Why do commitments matter to Finance?', 'They show future cost already promised, so forecasts and estimate-at-completion are realistic, and budgetary control can stop overspending.']}],
['Costing Sources', 'How do inventory and manufacturing costs reach a project?', `**Why they ask:** key for construction and engineer-to-order clients.

**Answer:**
"Material is received into project-specific inventory, so stock bought for one job isn't used by another. Cost Accounting values the receipt and later the issue to the job or to a project work order at the item's cost. A transfer process then sends those issued costs to Project Costing as expenditure items, already accounted by Cost Accounting. So the project sees material alongside AP, labor and expense costs."

Tip: Mention project-specific inventory. It shows you understand engineer-to-order.`, 'D', 'S', null, {f:['What happens to variances in a standard-cost organization?', 'Variances are accounted in Cost Accounting; whether they flow to the project depends on setup, so agree with Finance how project margin should treat them.']}],
['Costing Sources', 'How are labor costs brought into projects?', `**Why they ask:** labor is often the largest project cost.

**Answer:**
"Time cards in Time and Labor carry project and task. Project Costing imports them and applies a cost rate from labor costing rules or rate schedules, by person, job or organization. Projects then accounts the labor cost, typically debiting project cost and crediting a labor clearing account that is reconciled to payroll. Some clients bring actual payroll cost instead through Payroll costing distribution."

Tip: Mention reconciling labor clearing to payroll.`, 'D', 'M', null, {f:['Why use standard cost rates instead of actual payroll?', 'They are available immediately and stable for billing and forecasting; the difference to payroll stays in a clearing account that Finance reconciles.']}],
['Burdening', 'What is burdening and how is it accounted?', `**Why they ask:** overhead recovery on projects is a Finance topic.

**Answer:**
"Burdening adds indirect costs, such as fringe, overhead and G&A, to direct costs. A burden structure defines cost bases and the burden cost codes; burden schedules hold the rates by organization and date; the project type decides which schedule applies. Projects calculates burden on eligible expenditure items and can show it on the same line or as separate burden lines. Burden costs are accounted by Projects through SLA, typically debiting project cost and crediting an overhead absorption account."

Tip: Mention reconciling overhead absorbed versus actual overhead in GL.`, 'D', 'S', null, {f:['Burdening or GL allocations — when do you use which?', 'Burdening when overhead must sit on the project for billing, revenue or capitalization. GL allocations when overhead is only needed at management-reporting level.']}],
['Costing Sources', 'How do expense reports charge to projects?', `**Why they ask:** a smaller but common flow.

**Answer:**
"Employees enter project and task on the expense report. After approval and audit, the expense report becomes a Payables invoice and is accounted there. Import Costs brings the expense lines into Project Costing, already accounted. Transaction controls stop charges to closed or non-chargeable tasks."

Tip: Keep it short; it follows the AP path.`, 'B', 'M', null, {f:['Where do you stop wrong project charges on expenses?', 'At entry, using transaction controls and task chargeable flags, so errors don\'t reach AP or Projects.']}],
['Adjustments', 'A cost was charged to the wrong project. How do you fix it?', `**Why they ask:** tests whether you keep Projects and GL in sync.

**Answer:**
"I fix it in Projects, not GL: transfer the expenditure item to the right project and task, or adjust it. Projects reverses the original and creates the new item, and SLA creates the matching accounting if the account changes. This keeps the project view, the supporting reference balances and GL consistent. A GL-only journal would correct the account but leave the project wrong."

Tip: The rule: project adjustments go through Projects.`, 'B', 'M', null, {f:['What if the project is already billed?', 'Billing has to be adjusted too, through invoice credit or rebilling, and revenue recalculated. Check with the project accountant before transferring.']}],
['Capital Projects', 'How do capital projects send costs to Fixed Assets?', `**Why they ask:** CIP and capitalization are core Finance.

**Answer:**
"Capital projects collect costs as construction in process. Project assets are defined with category, book and location. When an asset is placed in service, Generate Asset Lines creates lines grouped by asset and Transfer to Fixed Assets sends them to Mass Additions. FA posts them and starts depreciation; CIP is relieved. I reconcile CIP in GL to capital project costs not yet capitalized every close."

Tip: Mention CIP reconciliation — auditors ask for it.`, 'D', 'S', null, {t:1, f:['How do you handle costs that shouldn\'t be capitalized?', 'Mark the expenditure items or tasks as non-capitalizable so they go to expense, or exclude them when generating asset lines.']}],
['SLA', 'How is the account derived for project costs and revenue?', `**Why they ask:** shows you understand the Finance engine under Projects.

**Answer:**
"Subledger Accounting builds the account from project attributes. Account rules use sources like expenditure type to derive the natural account, the project or expenditure organization to derive the cost center, and project type or capitalizable flag to choose expense, WIP or CIP. Mapping sets translate values, for example expenditure type to natural account. Users never type the account on project costs; they pick project, task and expenditure type."

Tip: Give one example: expenditure type Subcontractor maps to the subcontract cost account.`, 'D', 'S', null, {t:1, f:['How would you fill a cost center when project organizations don\'t match cost centers one to one?', 'Use a mapping set from organization to cost center, with a default value and an exception report, maintained as part of organization setup.']}],
['Close', 'How do Projects periods work with GL periods, and what is the close sequence?', `**Why they ask:** close coordination across modules.

**Answer:**
"Projects has accounting periods aligned with GL, and project accounting periods can be more granular if needed. The sequence is: close AP, receipts and Cost Accounting cut-off; import all costs into Projects; run burdening; generate revenue and invoices; create accounting for Projects and transfer to GL; review exceptions and reconcile; then close the project period before GL. Late costs go into the next open period."

Tip: Mention that revenue runs after all costs are in.`, 'D', 'S', null, {f:['Why must all costs be imported before revenue runs?', 'Percent-complete revenue uses cost to date; missing costs would understate revenue for the period.']}]
]);

// Part 2: contracts, billing, revenue, construction and engineer-to-order, control and close-out.
QB['ora-mod-ppm'] = (QB['ora-mod-ppm'] || []).concat([
['Billing & Revenue', 'How do Project Contracts and Project Billing work together?', `**Why they ask:** the revenue side of the suite.

**Answer:**
"A contract has a customer, lines and funding linked to projects or tasks. Each line has a bill plan, which decides how invoices are calculated, and a revenue plan, which decides how revenue is recognized. Billing methods include time and materials, cost-plus with markups, percent complete, and amount- or rate-based milestones. Invoices are generated, approved and sent to Receivables; revenue is generated and accounted through SLA to GL."

Tip: Separate bill plan from revenue plan. Billing and revenue often differ.`, 'B', 'M', null, {t:1, f:['Why keep bill plan and revenue plan separate?', 'Contracts often bill on milestones but recognize revenue over time; separating them handles ASC 606 correctly.']}],
['Billing & Revenue', 'Explain the billing methods: T&M, cost-plus, percent complete and milestone.', `**Why they ask:** construction and services use different ones.

**Answer:**
"Time and materials bills hours and costs at bill rates. Cost-plus bills actual cost with a markup or fee, common on cost-reimbursable contracts. Percent complete bills a share of the contract value based on progress, often cost-to-cost or physical progress. Milestone billing invoices fixed amounts when events are met. Construction often bills monthly progress against a schedule of values with retention held back."

Tip: Tie construction to progress billing plus retention.`, 'B', 'M', null, {f:['Which method gives the most cash-flow risk to the contractor?', 'Milestone billing, because cost is spent long before the milestone is reached and invoiced.']}],
['Billing & Revenue', 'How is revenue recognized on long construction contracts under ASC 606?', `**Why they ask:** the core Finance concept on project clients.

**Answer:**
"Most construction contracts meet over-time recognition because the customer controls the asset as it's built. The usual measure is cost-to-cost percent complete: cost to date divided by estimated total cost, times the contract value, less revenue recognized before. In Fusion this is a percent-complete revenue method on the revenue plan, using actual cost and the latest forecast. The estimate at completion must be current each month, or revenue is wrong."

Tip: Stress that a stale forecast means wrong revenue.`, 'D', 'S', null, {t:1, f:['What happens when the estimated total cost goes up?', 'Percent complete drops, so revenue is adjusted in the current period; if total cost exceeds the contract value, the full expected loss is recognized right away.']}],
['Billing & Revenue', 'Explain unbilled receivables and billings in excess, with the accounting.', `**Why they ask:** the heart of the construction WIP schedule.

**Answer:**
"When revenue recognized is more than billed, the difference is unbilled receivable, a contract asset: debit unbilled receivable, credit revenue. When billing is ahead of revenue, it's billings in excess, a contract liability: invoicing debits AR and credits unbilled first, then unearned revenue. Fusion manages both through Project Billing accounting. The WIP schedule compares the two by job every month."

Tip: Say over-billed equals liability, under-billed equals asset.`, 'D', 'S', null, {t:1, f:['Why do auditors look closely at under-billing?', 'Large under-billings can mean revenue recognized on optimistic estimates that may never be billed or collected.']}],
['Construction', 'How does retention (retainage) work on the owner side and subcontractor side?', `**Why they ask:** retention is everywhere in construction.

**Answer:**
"On the owner side, the contractor bills full progress but the owner withholds a percentage, often 5–10%. Project Billing supports retention terms, creating a retention receivable that is billed when released, often at completion. On the subcontractor side, Procurement complex work contracts withhold retention from subcontractor payments, recorded as retention payable and released at completion. Finance reports both by job."

Tip: Mention both sides — owner retention receivable, subcontractor retention payable.`, 'D', 'S', null, {f:['Where does retention show on the balance sheet?', 'Retention receivable is a contract asset or receivable; retention payable is a liability, both tracked by job.']}],
['Construction', 'How are subcontractor payments handled in Fusion?', `**Why they ask:** subcontractors are a big share of construction cost.

**Answer:**
"Procurement complex work contracts hold the schedule of values, progress payment terms, retention and advances with recoupment. The subcontractor submits progress, which becomes a pay request and Payables invoice for the work done, less retention and recouped advances. The invoice carries project details, so the cost flows to Project Costing. Lien waivers and compliance documents are often handled in Textura Payment Management, integrated with ERP."

Tip: Mention Textura if the client is a large general contractor.`, 'D', 'S', null, {f:['What compliance checks happen before paying a subcontractor?', 'Lien waivers, insurance certificates, licensing and approved progress, usually as holds before payment.']}],
['Billing & Revenue', 'How do project invoices get to Receivables?', `**Why they ask:** the AR integration.

**Answer:**
"After invoices are generated and approved in Project Billing, Transfer to Receivables sends them through the AutoInvoice interface. Receivables creates the transactions linked to the contract and project, accounts the receivable, and handles receipts and collections. The invoice keeps the project reference, so project billed amounts reconcile to AR."

Tip: Mention reconciling project billed amounts to AR.`, 'B', 'M', null, {f:['An invoice was transferred but didn\'t appear in AR. What do you check?', 'AutoInvoice errors and the Transfer to Receivables output, usually customer, transaction type or accounting setup problems.']}],
['Construction', 'What is a WIP schedule and how do you produce it from Fusion?', `**Why they ask:** a key construction finance report, also used by banks and sureties.

**Answer:**
"The WIP schedule lists each job: contract value, estimated total cost, cost to date, percent complete, revenue earned, billed to date, and over- or under-billing, plus estimated gross profit. In Fusion the data comes from Project Contracts, Costing, Control forecasts and Billing. I build it in OTBI or BIP, or use Construction Intelligence, and reconcile totals to the GL unbilled and billings-in-excess accounts."

Tip: Mention sureties and banks — they rely on it.`, 'D', 'S', null, {t:1, f:['What does margin fade mean?', 'When estimated gross profit on a job keeps dropping from month to month — an early sign of a problem job.']}],
['Engineer to Order', 'What is engineer-to-order, and how is it different from make-to-stock and configure-to-order?', `**Why they ask:** ETO and project-driven supply chain are often in construction and engineering JDs.

**Answer:**
"Make-to-stock builds standard products before orders. Configure-to-order assembles standard options chosen by the customer. Engineer-to-order designs the product for a specific customer after the contract, like custom machinery, power equipment, ships or large structures, often over months or years. In Oracle that's project-driven supply chain: the contract and project come first, and requisitions, POs, work orders and inventory all carry the project."

Tip: Give one example from an industry you know.`, 'B', 'M', null, {t:1, f:['Why does ETO need project-specific inventory?', 'Material is engineered and bought for one job, so it must be reserved and costed to that job, not used elsewhere.']}],
['Engineer to Order', 'Walk me through the end-to-end flow of an engineer-to-order job in Oracle.', `**Why they ask:** tests whether you can connect SCM, Projects and Finance.

**Answer:**
"A contract and project are created with tasks for design, procurement, manufacturing and installation. Engineering creates items and the bill of materials. Requisitions and POs carry the project; receipts go into project inventory; work orders consume that material. Cost Accounting values it and transfers cost to Project Costing; AP, labor and expenses come in directly. Project Billing invoices by milestone or progress; revenue is recognized over time. Finance sees margin and WIP by job."

Tip: End with "the project is where cost, billing and margin come together."`, 'D', 'S', null, {f:['Where do things usually go wrong in this flow?', 'Material bought without the project, so it lands in common inventory, and late forecasts that make percent-complete revenue wrong.']}],
['Construction', 'What applications are in Oracle\'s construction stack, and how do they connect to Fusion?', `**Why they ask:** construction clients rarely use Fusion alone.

**Answer:**
"Primavera P6 or Primavera Cloud handles schedules and progress; Unifier handles owner-side cost controls, change orders and pay applications; Aconex handles documents and RFIs; Textura handles subcontractor pay apps and lien waivers. Fusion Project Financials is the system of record for cost, billing and revenue. Typical integrations through OIC: project structure and WBS synced with Primavera, percent complete back to Fusion for revenue and billing, and committed and actual cost sent to the scheduling tool."

Tip: Say which system owns what — schedule versus financials.`, 'D', 'S', null, {t:1, f:['Which system should own the WBS?', 'Usually the scheduling tool owns the detailed schedule; Fusion owns the financial task structure. Agree a mapping at the level both need.']}],
['Project Control', 'How do budgets, forecasts and estimate-at-completion work in Projects?', `**Why they ask:** revenue and margin depend on them.

**Answer:**
"Project Control holds an approved cost and revenue budget, often by task and resource, with versions. Forecasts give estimate to complete and estimate at completion, usually monthly. Actuals and commitments are compared to both. For percent-complete revenue, the cost forecast drives the estimate at completion, so its accuracy directly affects revenue."

Tip: Stress monthly forecast discipline.`, 'B', 'M', null, {f:['What is budgetary control on a project?', 'Checking requisitions, POs and invoices against available project budget and stopping or warning when funds run out.']}],
['Project Control', 'How does a change order affect the contract, budget and revenue?', `**Why they ask:** change orders are constant in construction.

**Answer:**
"An approved change order amends the contract value and the cost budget. In Fusion, the contract is amended with a new version and the budget and forecast are revised. Percent-complete revenue then recalculates with the new contract value and estimated cost. Unapproved change orders are tracked separately and only included in revenue when they're probable and enforceable under ASC 606."

Tip: Mention unapproved change orders — auditors focus on them.`, 'D', 'S', null, {f:['What controls do you put around change orders?', 'Approval workflow, versioned contract amendments, and a log reconciling approved changes to contract value.']}],
['Billing & Revenue', 'How do you handle a loss-making project?', `**Why they ask:** a key accounting rule for contracts.

**Answer:**
"When the estimated total cost exceeds total revenue, the whole expected loss is recognized immediately, not spread over the job. In Fusion that usually means a loss provision entry by job, either through a Projects adjustment or a controlled GL entry with the project as a reference, based on the forecast. Finance reviews loss jobs monthly."

Tip: Say "full loss recognized immediately."`, 'D', 'S', null, {f:['How do you spot a loss job early?', 'Margin fade on the WIP schedule and forecasts rising faster than progress.']}],
['Close-out', 'What happens when a project closes out?', `**Why they ask:** end-of-life Finance steps.

**Answer:**
"Final costs are collected and accruals cleared; final invoice and retention billing are issued; revenue is trued up to the final contract value; for capital projects, remaining CIP is capitalized; and the project status changes to closed so no new charges come in. Finance confirms unbilled, retention and WIP balances for the job are zero or explained."

Tip: Check that job balances clear to zero.`, 'B', 'M', null, {f:['What balances most often remain after close-out?', 'Retention receivable or payable not yet released, and small unbilled or accrual differences.']}]
]);

// Part 3: SLA in depth, intercompany and cross-charges, GL allocations, reporting, reconciliation, conversions and integrations.
QB['ora-mod-ppm'] = (QB['ora-mod-ppm'] || []).concat([
['SLA', 'Explain how Subledger Accounting is built: event classes, sources, rules and methods.', `**Why they ask:** a Finance lead must explain SLA clearly, especially for Projects.

**Answer:**
"Each subledger raises accounting events, grouped into event classes and types, such as Labor Cost or Revenue in Projects. Sources are the data fields the event provides, like project, expenditure type or organization. Account rules use sources and conditions to build accounts, or segment values, often through mapping sets. Journal line rules define debit or credit, the accounting class and supporting references. Description rules build journal text. These are combined in a journal entry rule set per event class, and the rule sets roll up into the subledger accounting method assigned to the ledger."

Tip: Use the order: events → sources → rules → JLT → rule set → method → ledger.`, 'D', 'S', null, {t:1, f:['What is the difference between an account rule and a mapping set?', 'An account rule decides how an account or segment is built; a mapping set is a lookup table it can use, like expenditure type to natural account.']}],
['SLA', 'How would you change SLA so project costs derive the cost center from the project organization?', `**Why they ask:** a practical SLA design question.

**Answer:**
"I copy the seeded Projects accounting method, because seeded components can't be edited. I create a mapping set from project organization to cost center, then a segment rule for the cost center segment using that mapping set. I attach the rule to the relevant journal entry rule sets, for example labor and miscellaneous cost, activate them, and assign the new method to the ledger. Then I test with Create Accounting in draft against an expected-entries matrix before final."

Tip: Mention copying seeded rules — interviewers check for it.`, 'D', 'S', null, {f:['What happens if the mapping set has no value?', 'Use a default value or let accounting fail into an error report; defaults are safer, with a report to catch them.']}],
['SLA', 'How does Projects accounting get to GL — create accounting, summarization and drill-down?', `**Why they ask:** practical knowledge of the posting flow.

**Answer:**
"Create Accounting for Projects runs by process category, like costs or revenue, in draft or final mode. Final entries are transferred to GL and can be posted automatically. Journal import can summarize lines by account to keep GL small, but the detail stays in SLA, so users drill from GL journal to the subledger entry and then to the expenditure item or invoice."

Tip: Say summarization doesn't lose detail — it stays in SLA.`, 'B', 'M', null, {f:['How do you check what hasn\'t been accounted yet?', 'Run the subledger period close exceptions report and unaccounted transactions reports before closing the period.']}],
['Intercompany', 'What is the difference between intercompany balancing rules and intercompany transactions in Fusion?', `**Why they ask:** a common confusion and a must-know for Finance.

**Answer:**
"Balancing rules work automatically inside one journal: when a journal hits two primary balancing segment values, GL or SLA adds due-to and due-from lines so each balances. Intercompany transactions are actual business transactions between legal entities, like charges for services, created in the Intercompany module with approval, and optionally turned into AR and AP invoices when the entities need formal invoices for tax or legal reasons."

Tip: Balancing = automatic in a journal; transactions = formal, between entities.`, 'D', 'S', null, {t:1, f:['When must you use AR and AP invoicing instead of just balancing?', 'When entities are separate legal companies that need invoices for tax, VAT or transfer pricing, or settle cash between them.']}],
['Intercompany', 'How are intercompany balancing rules built and which one wins?', `**Why they ask:** tests how the setup actually works.

**Answer:**
"Rules can be defined at four levels: primary balancing segment, legal entity, ledger, and chart of accounts. Each rule gives the receivable and payable accounts, by source and category or for all. Fusion checks the most specific rule first: primary balancing segment value rules, then legal entity, then ledger, then chart of accounts, so a chart-level rule acts as the default. If an entity also uses clearing company options, those decide which entity is the counterparty when a journal has many balancing values."

Tip: Remember the order: segment → legal entity → ledger → chart.`, 'D', 'S', null, {t:1, f:['What is a clearing company?', 'A balancing value used as the counterparty when one journal crosses many entities, so you don\'t get many-to-many due-to and due-from lines.']}],
['Intercompany', 'How do you set up intercompany transactions in Fusion?', `**Why they ask:** the module setup steps.

**Answer:**
"Define intercompany system options such as minimum amount, currency, conversion rate type and whether invoicing is required; create intercompany organizations linked to legal entities and receivables and payables business units; define transaction types with approval and invoicing flags; set intercompany accounts; and open intercompany periods. Users then create a batch, the receiver approves, and the system creates GL journals or AR and AP invoices."

Tip: Mention receiver approval — it reduces disputes.`, 'D', 'S', null, {f:['How do you reduce intercompany disputes at month-end?', 'Receiver approval, agreed cut-off dates, and reconciling by counterparty before close.']}],
['Intercompany', 'How do project cross-charges work when one business unit charges another business unit\'s project?', `**Why they ask:** Projects plus intercompany is common in large companies.

**Answer:**
"Cross-charge happens when a resource from one business unit or legal entity works on a project owned by another. Options are: no cross-charge; borrowed and lent accounting, which moves cost between organizations in the same legal entity; or intercompany billing, where the provider issues an internal invoice to the receiver through Project Billing, AR and AP, because they're different legal entities. Transfer price rules set the markup."

Tip: Borrowed and lent within a legal entity; intercompany billing across legal entities.`, 'D', 'S', null, {t:1, f:['Why not just use balancing rules for cross-charges between legal entities?', 'Because separate legal entities often need a real invoice and a transfer-price markup for tax; balancing rules only create due-to and due-from entries.']}],
['Intercompany', 'How do you reconcile intercompany balances?', `**Why they ask:** a key close control.

**Answer:**
"I run the Intercompany Reconciliation Report, which matches receivable and payable balances by entity pair, and drill to differences. Typical causes are timing, currency rates, one side not approved or posted, or wrong accounts. Reconciliation is a close step before consolidation, with agreed tolerances and owners."

Tip: Mention doing it before consolidation, not after.`, 'B', 'M', null, {f:['What is the most common cause of differences?', 'Timing: one entity has booked the charge and the other hasn\'t, often around cut-off.']}],
['Allocations', 'How are allocation rules built in Fusion GL?', `**Why they ask:** allocations are named often in Finance interviews.

**Answer:**
"Allocations use Calculation Manager against the GL balances cube. A rule has a point of view for fixed dimensions like ledger, period and scenario; a source, the pool to allocate, such as IT cost center expenses; a basis, the driver such as headcount or square feet, often from statistical accounts; a target, where amounts go; and an offset, which credits the source. Rules can use run-time prompts for period and are grouped into rule sets, run in sequence for step-down allocations. Generate General Ledger Allocations creates the journal, which can post automatically."

Tip: Remember: point of view, source, basis, target, offset.`, 'D', 'S', null, {t:1, f:['How do you keep allocation drivers current?', 'Load statistical balances like headcount each month before running allocations, with a check report.']}],
['Allocations', 'Give an example of an allocation rule you would build.', `**Why they ask:** they want to see you apply it.

**Answer:**
"Allocating IT costs to business cost centers by headcount. Point of view: ledger, current period, actual. Source: all IT cost center expense accounts. Basis: statistical headcount by cost center. Target: each cost center, to an IT allocation expense account. Offset: an IT allocation credit account in the IT cost center. I test in a test period, check the source nets to zero and targets add up, then schedule it in the close calendar."

Tip: Mention checking that source equals targets.`, 'B', 'M', null, {f:['How would you do a step-down allocation?', 'Order the rules in a rule set so the first pool allocates, then the next pool includes what it received, run in sequence.']}],
['Allocations', 'When do you use GL allocations versus project burdening?', `**Why they ask:** shows judgment across modules.

**Answer:**
"Burdening applies overhead at the expenditure-item level, so it lands on the project and affects billing, revenue and capitalization, which is right for cost-plus contracts or government work. GL allocations work on balances at summary level and are right for management reporting of shared costs. If overhead must reach the job, use burdening; otherwise, allocations are simpler."

Tip: Ask: does the overhead need to be on the job?`, 'D', 'S', null, {f:['Can you use both?', 'Yes — burdening for project overhead recovery and GL allocations for remaining shared costs, with a reconciliation of overhead absorbed.']}],
['Reconciliation', 'How do you reconcile Projects to GL?', `**Why they ask:** a control Finance owns.

**Answer:**
"By period, I compare project cost, revenue, unbilled and billings in excess in Projects against the GL control accounts, using supporting reference balances and Projects-to-GL reconciliation reports. Before close I clear unaccounted transactions and import exceptions. Differences are usually GL manual journals or costs not yet imported."

Tip: Mention both unaccounted transactions and import exceptions.`, 'D', 'S', null, {t:1, f:['Why might AP cost be in GL but not in Projects?', 'The invoice was accounted in AP but Import Costs hasn\'t run or failed for those distributions.']}],
['Reporting', 'What project reports does Finance usually need, and where do they come from?', `**Why they ask:** practical reporting knowledge.

**Answer:**
"Job cost by task and expenditure type, budget versus actual and forecast, commitments, revenue and billing, WIP and over/under-billing, margin by job, retention, and capital project CIP. They come from OTBI Projects subject areas for self-service, BIP for formatted reports, and Construction Intelligence or Fusion Data Intelligence for trends and dashboards, reconciled to GL."

Tip: Name the WIP schedule first for construction clients.`, 'B', 'M', null, {f:['Which reports would you show a CFO?', 'WIP schedule, margin by job and fade trends, backlog, and cash: billings, collections and retention.']}],
['Conversion', 'How would you convert open projects from a legacy system into Fusion?', `**Why they ask:** conversions often fall to the Finance lead.

**Answer:**
"I load project structures and contracts with FBDI or APIs, then summarized cost to date by task and expenditure type as accounted cost, so it doesn't post again to GL; revenue and billing to date; open AR invoices and retention; budgets and forecasts; and open commitments. GL balances convert separately. I reconcile project totals to legacy and to GL after each mock conversion."

Tip: Converted costs come in as already accounted — say so.`, 'D', 'S', null, {f:['Do you convert transaction-level history?', 'Usually not; summarized cost by task and type is enough, with detail kept in an archive.']}],
['Integration', 'How would you integrate Primavera with Fusion Projects?', `**Why they ask:** common on construction and engineering programs.

**Answer:**
"Through OIC, with Oracle's prebuilt integrations where available. Fusion creates the project and financial task structure, which is sent to Primavera; Primavera builds the detailed schedule and sends progress or percent complete back to Fusion for revenue and billing; Fusion sends committed and actual cost to Primavera for earned value. I agree the task mapping level, frequency and error handling."

Tip: Agree who owns the WBS before designing the interface.`, 'D', 'S', null, {f:['What usually breaks this integration?', 'Task structures getting out of sync when one side adds or renames tasks without the other.']}],
['Security', 'How is data access secured in Projects?', `**Why they ask:** controls and SOX.

**Answer:**
"Job roles like project accountant, billing specialist and project manager give functions. Data access is by business unit for financial roles and by project role for project managers, so managers see their projects. I check segregation of duties, such as the same person creating contracts and approving invoices, and test with real users in UAT."

Tip: Mention project manager access by project role.`, 'B', 'M', null, {f:['Which SOD conflict matters most in Project Billing?', 'One person creating contract billing terms and also approving and releasing invoices.']}],
['Situational', 'Project costs are in Projects but not in GL. How do you troubleshoot?', `**Why they ask:** a real support scenario.

**Answer:**
"I check whether the costs come from a source Projects accounts, like labor or burden; if so, run Create Accounting for Projects and check errors in the output and unaccounted transactions report. Common causes are account rule failures, closed GL periods, or cross-validation errors. If the costs came from AP or receipts, they're accounted in those modules instead, so I check there."

Tip: Start by asking which module should have accounted the cost.`, 'B', 'M', null, {f:['What is the fix for a cross-validation error in project accounting?', 'Correct the account rule or mapping set so it builds a valid combination, then rerun accounting.']}],
['Role Fit', 'You are a Finance lead, not a Projects consultant. Why does Projects knowledge matter to you?', `**Why they ask:** they want confidence that you can work across the line.

**Answer:**
"In a project-driven business, most of the GL is fed by Projects: cost, revenue, unbilled and billings in excess, retention and CIP. I need to design the chart, SLA, intercompany and reporting so Projects and Finance reconcile, and run a close that depends on Projects processes. I don't configure Projects, but I own how it lands in Finance and I partner with the Projects lead on everything that touches accounting."

Tip: Use "I own how Projects lands in Finance."`, 'B', 'S', null, {t:1, f:['Where would you draw the line with the Projects consultant?', 'They own project setup, billing rules and planning; I own accounting rules, GL, intercompany, reconciliation and close, and we co-own revenue and capitalization design.']}]
]);

QB['ora-mod-ppm'] = (QB['ora-mod-ppm'] || []).concat([["Projects","What does \"projects linked manufacturing\" mean, and how have you supported it?","**Why they ask:** a specific JD skill. Shows depth beyond core Finance.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It's when manufacturing work is tied to a project, often engineer-to-order or large CTO deals. Work orders and purchase orders carry project and task references, so material and labor costs flow from Costing into Project Costing. Billing comes from Project Billing or contracts, often by milestone, and revenue may use percent complete. My role was designing accounting and costs flow to projects, setting up project types and expenditure types, and building margin reporting by project.\"\n\nTip: If your depth is limited, say what you did and how you partnered with SCM and PPM leads.","D","S",null,{"f":["How do you make sure project costs and inventory costs reconcile?","Reconcile WIP and project cost by project each close, and use cost transfer reports to see what has not moved to projects."]}],["Projects","How does Fusion Project Financials integrate with Financials?","**Why they ask:** integration knowledge across Projects and Finance.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Costs come into Project Costing from AP invoices, POs, expenses, time and inventory, with project and task on the source. Project Costing creates accounting through SLA. Project Billing creates invoices to AR, and revenue posts to GL. Capital projects send assets to FA. I make sure expenditure types map to the right accounts and that project references reach the GL for reporting.\"\n\nTip: Mention capital projects to FA.","B","S",null,{"f":["What do you check when project costs are missing?","Whether the source transaction had project details, whether imports and cost collection processes ran, and errors in the Import Costs or Unprocessed Transactions reports."]}],["Projects","How does Fusion Project Financials interact with core Financials?","**Why they ask:** projects touch most of finance.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Project costing collects costs from AP invoices, expenses, time, inventory and POs against projects and tasks. Capital projects generate CIP assets and send them to Assets. Project billing and contracts create invoices through AR, and revenue is recognized with project revenue methods. Burdening adds overheads, and cross-charging moves costs between organizations. Accounting goes through SLA for costing and billing. A common design point is keeping projects out of the chart of accounts and tracking them as a subledger attribute instead.\"\n\nTip: Make the \"don't put projects in the COA\" point. It's a classic design mistake.","D",null,null,{"f":["Where do capital project costs end up?","In CIP assets, then capitalized to fixed assets when the project is placed in service."]}]]);
